# Trivy ignore file
# Add CVE IDs (one per line) to suppress specific vulnerabilities.
#
# Every entry MUST carry:
#   - why it is accepted (unfixed upstream, unreachable in this context, ...)
#   - the GitHub code-scanning alert number, where one exists
#   - a "Revisit YYYY-MM-DD" date
#
# The revisit date is the control that stops a suppression becoming permanent.
# scripts/check-trivyignore-expiry.sh fails once a date has passed, so an
# expired entry breaks the security workflow instead of ageing quietly.
#
# Format:
#   CVE-2024-XXXXX  # accepted: <reason>. GitHub alert #NNN. Revisit YYYY-MM-DD.
#
# Reference: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/#by-finding-ids

# lopdf 0.38.0 / 0.39.0 in the memvid binary: stack overflow via deeply nested
# PDF objects. GitHub published this on 2026-10-16 as GHSA-6fj4-rcr3-r7rr; it
# is the same advisory as RUSTSEC-2026-0187, already accepted for `cargo audit`
# in memvid-service/.cargo/audit.toml, and the reasoning is the same:
# memvid-core 2.0.140 (still the latest release) caps lopdf ^0.39, the fix is
# 0.42.0 (memvid/memvid#242), and this service opens a pre-built .mv2 read-only
# -- it never parses a PDF at serving time. Trivy now lists a fixed version, so
# --ignore-unfixed no longer skips it. No code-scanning alert; Dependabot alert
# #132 was dismissed as tolerable_risk on 2026-10-25 (see docs/SECURITY.md).
GHSA-6fj4-rcr3-r7rr  # accepted: lopdf via memvid-core cap, PDF parser unreachable at serving time. Revisit 2026-11-24.

# History: the seven libc6/Trixie entries that lived here were all inherited from the
# memvid runtime when it was based on distroless/cc-debian13. That base was
# replaced with distroless/static in #496, which removed libc entirely, so the
# findings no longer exist to suppress. Verified against every current runtime
# base and against a fully built api-service image: all report zero
# vulnerabilities, and none of the seven CVEs appear anywhere.
#
# They were removed rather than re-dated: a suppression for a finding that
# cannot occur is not protection, it is a blind spot. If any of them resurface
# under a future base image, Trivy should fail loudly rather than stay quiet.
